---
// src/pages/uis/index.astro  →  arizvi.com/uis
// For the UIS search (Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor, Engineering Technology; founding faculty for the BAS-ET).
// The page follows Ali's own framing (6 Nov 2026): "Building a program using my research skills, teaching it and
// measuring it longitudinally." The copy comes from his UIS cover letter, teaching philosophy and statement of
// professional practice (15 Oct 2026). Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
// UIS facts are from the posting (Req 1288), the Board of Trustees item of 19 March 2026 and UIS's 2027-28 calendar
// (15 weeks of classes, then finals). The course is a proposal and is labelled as one; the numbers in its labs are
// invented so they can be checked.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import ClassPlan from '../../components/ClassPlan.astro';
import GaugeDemo from '../../components/GaugeDemo.astro';
import { classes } from '../../data/classes';

const plan = [
  {
    label: 'Build it',
    title: 'With my research skills',
    line: 'Many students will arrive from community colleges or from work. Before the first cohort, I\'d interview the employers who will host them and the community college faculty who teach them, and map the courses that feed the program, as I mapped the design sequence at Waterloo. Each student then starts from what they can already do. From the first term I\'d take on the industry advisory board and the community college pathways.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Teach it',
    title: 'Hands-on, in the lab',
    line: 'Students take a system from requirement to tested build, managing the project as they go, then work an employer\'s problem through the work-based learning sequence.',
    link: { href: '#areas', text: 'What I\'d teach is below' },
  },
  {
    label: 'Measure it',
    title: 'Longitudinally',
    line: 'The same measures for every cohort, from the first course to the first job. I compare course designs across cohorts, share the results with colleagues teaching the same students, and carry the stronger design into the next term. It\'s also the evidence ABET ETAC accreditation asks for, and I\'d start that work with ABET\'s own training on the criteria.',
  },
];

const principles = [
  ['Build the course backwards from the outcome.', 'I start each course from where it fits in the program, where it falls in the students\' path through their degree, and where they\'ll use it afterwards. Then I set what students should be able to do by the end of term.'],
  ['Personalize within a defined structure.', 'Everyone runs the same labs. Within that, I adjust to the cohort in front of me: Figma training for a studio cohort new to it, Python and data visualization modules for a graduate cohort whose plans leaned toward data.'],
  ['Learn on real problems.', 'Students understand a system best when they build and test it where it will be used. In the lab they work from written procedures and log every test, so safety and documentation are habits before they reach an employer.'],
  ['Test the teaching itself.', 'Open bidding for project groups left teams unbalanced, so I moved to matching students on ranked topic and skill set.'],
];

const areas = [
  {
    role: 'I\'d teach',
    title: 'Systems integration, project management and applied engineering practice',
    line: 'Systems integration runs through my industry work. At Amazon I owned the orchestration layer that routes each Alexa request and carries context across turns, and at Morgan Stanley I implemented a Hadoop-based data lake with partners in IT. I\'ve built hardware too: SecureCheck-N, an infrared temperature-screening kiosk that connects over Bluetooth to any tablet or phone, went from concept to manufacturing in 3 weeks and is ISO and UL certified. Students would take a system from a requirement to a tested, documented build, managing scope, schedule and risk as they go, and hand it over to the people who will run it.',
    link: { href: '#course', text: 'The course I\'d build first is below' },
  },
  {
    role: 'I\'d coordinate',
    title: 'Work-based learning and industry partnerships',
    line: 'I ran 3 sections of the client-facing capstone, with teams delivering working prototypes to external clients such as Rogers, and I\'d build the work-based learning sequence the same way. In industry I built teams and owned relationships from the C-suite down, and I\'d bring that to the advisory board and the employers who host BAS-ET students.',
    link: { href: '#wbl', text: 'How I\'d run a placement is below' },
  },
  {
    role: 'My third area',
    title: 'Measurement and quality systems',
    line: 'Human-Computer Interaction, Analytics and User Experience, and Introduction to UX all taught data analytics and statistics. My industry work turned on measurement: A/B tests decided which Alexa features shipped to over 100 million people, and the testing strategy I designed at PwC and EY closed a $600 million regulatory matter for a global bank. Students would design a measurement, test a system against its specification, and monitor it for drift.',
    link: { href: '#try', text: 'Try the gauge class below' },
  },
];

// The course I'd offer to build first: 15 weeks (UIS's fall term), each adding a piece of the final deliverable.
const weeks = [
  { w: 'Weeks 1-2', title: 'What a technical project is', line: 'Scope, requirements, constraints and stakeholders. Lab and site safety come first: nobody works at a bench or visits an employer until the university\'s safety orientation is signed off and on file. Students write a 1-page requirements sheet for a small device from a user interview.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 3-4', title: 'Design to cost', line: 'Bills of materials, trade-offs, make or buy. Lab 1: a temperature-screening kiosk\'s bill of materials comes to $90 a unit against a $100 target, and marketing wants a battery pack (+$25) and a metal stand (+$18). Neither fits in the $10 that\'s left, so teams decide what to cut, stay at or under $100 and defend 3 trade-offs.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 5-6', title: 'Schedules', line: 'Critical path, buffers and the weekly stand-up. Students mark the critical path on their own schedule and keep a stand-up sheet.' },
  { w: 'Week 7', title: 'Review and midterm', line: 'On paper: a project memo written from a scenario.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 8-9', title: 'Measurement and quality', line: 'Can the gauge be trusted (a gauge R&R study), then control charts. Lab 2 is the class at the bottom of this page.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 10-11', title: 'Systems integration', line: 'Interfaces, test plans and failure modes. Students write an interface list and an integration test plan.' },
  { w: 'Week 12', title: 'Ethics, product safety and societal impact', line: 'A 1-page decision record with the trade-off written down.' },
  { w: 'Weeks 13-15', title: 'The employer project', line: 'The charter and the plan, presented to the employer\'s supervisor, and a short reflection.' },
];

---
<Base title="UIS" description="How Ali Rizvi would build, teach and measure the Bachelor of Applied Science in Engineering Technology at the University of Illinois Springfield." noindex>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="label">UIS · College of Health, Science, and Technology</p>
        <h1>Building engineering technology at UIS</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="photo"><img src="/media/headshot.jpg" alt="Ali Rizvi" width="400" height="500" /></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <Section label="The program" id="program">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>I'd build the program using my research skills, teach it, and measure it longitudinally.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="rows bk-wide">
      {plan.map((p) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{p.label}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{p.title}</span>
            <p>{p.line}</p>
            {p.link && <p class="sample"><a href={p.link.href}>{p.link.text}</a></p>}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="How I teach" id="approach">
    <ol class="principles">
      {principles.map(([head, line]) => <li><strong>{head}</strong> {line}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p class="muted principles-note">BTech in Industrial Engineering, MS in Engineering Management Systems (Columbia, 2012) and PhD in Management Science and Engineering (Waterloo, 2025). 14 course sections of 6 courses at Waterloo between 2021 and 2024, over 700 students, up to 4 sections and 180 students in a term, in person, hybrid and online. My years at PwC, EY, Morgan Stanley and Amazon were technical leadership roles, and they give students worked examples: <a href="/work/amazon/">Alexa's orchestration layer</a>, <a href="/work/secure-check-n/">SecureCheck-N</a> and the <a href="/work/scramble/">Proteus card deck</a>. More on the <a href="/teaching/">Teaching</a> page.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section label="What I'd teach" id="areas">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Of the posting's 5 areas, I'd teach in 2 and contribute to a third.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="rows bk-wide">
      {areas.map((a) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{a.role}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{a.title}</span>
            <p>{a.line}</p>
            {a.link && <p class="sample"><a href={a.link.href}>{a.link.text}</a></p>}
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="A course I'd build first · a proposal" title="Engineering project management and systems integration" id="course">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>The course I'd offer to build first, under whatever title it carries there: 15 weeks from a requirement to a project charter and an integration test plan for an employer's problem.</p>
    </div></div>
    <ul class="rows compact bk-wide">
      {weeks.map((k) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">{k.w}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{k.title}</span>
            <p>{k.line}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">The numbers in the labs are invented so they can be checked. Labs are pass or redo, with feedback inside a week, and the midterm is on paper.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Work-based learning" title="How I'd run a placement" id="wbl">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Each student gets an employer's problem, a faculty adviser and an employer supervisor. It starts with a 1-page charter, signed by week 3 by the student, the supervisor and me. It says:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The problem, in the employer's words.</li>
        <li>Who the supervisor is, and how often they meet the student.</li>
        <li>What the student gets access to (equipment, data, the site), and the site's safety rules.</li>
        <li>What the employer gets back, and when.</li>
        <li>How we'll know it worked.</li>
        <li>The dates of the midterm check-in and the final presentation.</li>
        <li>A plan B if the problem changes.</li>
      </ol>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em;">The charter is a learning plan. The agreement between UIS and the employer (insurance, confidentiality, who owns the work, safety on site) is the university's, and it comes first.</p>
      <p>Then a 15-minute stand-up every week, a working result by midterm, and a final presentation with the employer in the room. Grades are for the plan, the delivery, and what the student learned when the plan met reality.</p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · week 8 of the course above" title="Is the gauge lying?" id="try">
    <ClassPlan plan={classes.gauge} course="uis" pack={false}>
      <p>A shop machines a part that has to come out within 0.25 mm of its nominal size, either way. That's a band 0.50 mm wide, and every part is checked with digital calipers before it ships.</p>
      <p>In class, 3 students measure 10 parts twice each with the same calipers. It's a gauge repeatability and reproducibility study (gauge R&amp;R). A perfect measurement system would give 1 part the same reading every time. Theirs doesn't, and how much the readings of 1 part disagree, within a student (repeatability) and between students (reproducibility), is the standard deviation of the measurement system: the calipers and the hands holding them.</p>
      <p>The number we work out is 6 of those standard deviations as a share of the band (gauge R&amp;R as a percentage of the tolerance). The usual guideline, from the AIAG measurement systems analysis manual: under 10% is acceptable, 10 to 30% may be acceptable depending on the job, and over 30% is unacceptable. The study measures scatter. Whether the calipers read true is a separate check against a gauge block, and the class does it first.</p>
      <p>The widget is the debrief. Say the class's calipers come out around 0.04 mm (an invented number, like the ones in the labs above). The room has to decide whether parts measured this way can ship.</p>
    </ClassPlan>
    <GaugeDemo />
  </Section>
</Base>
